<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>nossl.sh HTTP-only disclaimer</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Official HTTP-only disclaimer: nossl.sh will always stay plain HTTP - no SSL, no TLS, no HTTPS - on purpose for captive portal troubleshooting."
    />
    <meta
      name="keywords"
      content="nossl disclaimer, http only, no ssl, no tls, no https, captive portal testing, plain http"
    />
    <link rel="canonical" href="<%= canonicalUrl %>" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="nossl.sh HTTP-only disclaimer" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="This site will always be plain HTTP forever — no SSL, no TLS, no HTTPS — so captive portals and diagnostics stay predictable."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="<%= canonicalUrl %>" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="nossl.sh" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="/static/og-image.png" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Abstract teal and navy banner for nossl.sh" />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="nossl.sh HTTP-only disclaimer" />
    <meta
      name="twitter:description"
      content="Always forever plain HTTP—no SSL, no TLS, no HTTPS. This page explains why."
    />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="/static/og-image.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="Abstract teal and navy banner for nossl.sh" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/styles.css?v=2" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <%
      const formattedGeneratedAt = generatedAt.toISOString().replace('T', ' ').replace(/\..+/, ' UTC');
      const headerNote = 'Forever plain HTTP. No TLS. No HTTPS.';
      const footerLines = [
        'Need the diagnostics dashboard? <a href="/">Return to the main nossl.sh page</a> or open the <a href="/honeypot">honeypot console</a>.'
      ];
      const normalizedScheme = (scheme || 'http').toLowerCase();
      const isHttps = normalizedScheme === 'https';
    %>
    <%- include('partials/header', {headerNote}) %>

    <main class="container marketing">
      <article class="panel panel--connection">
        <header class="panel__header">
          <div>
            <h1>HTTP-only disclaimer</h1>
            <p class="panel__summary">
              nossl.sh will always stay plain HTTP - no SSL, no TLS, no HTTPS - on purpose.
            </p>
          </div>
        </header>
        <div class="panel__body">
          <p>
            nossl.sh exists to surface captive portals and inspect network behavior. To keep that predictable, everything here
            lives on raw port 80 with zero TLS, zero HSTS, and zero HTTPS redirects.
          </p>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>No SSL certificates now or later; no TLS handshakes or HTTPS endpoints at all.</li>
            <li>No auto-redirects, no mixed modes, no accidental HSTS or preload flags.</li>
            <li>Purpose-built for portal triggers, header checks, and IP lookups - not for sensitive data.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="muted">
            Current connection: <span class="mono"><%= normalizedScheme.toUpperCase() %></span>.
            <%= isHttps
              ? 'If you see HTTPS, a proxy is rewriting traffic - use http://nossl.sh directly for the intended behavior.'
              : 'You are already on the intended plain HTTP path.' %>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <section class="panel panel--support">
        <div class="panel__body">
          <article class="marketing-section">
            <h3>Why stay HTTP-only?</h3>
            <p>
              Captive portals and strict firewalls often block TLS handshakes, hide SNI, or break HSTS preloads. Staying on
              plain HTTP keeps splash screens, login prompts, and diagnostics predictable for every device.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="marketing-section">
            <h3>What you should expect</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>No encryption. Everything you send can be observed by the network.</li>
              <li>If a client forces HTTPS, that client or your network is rewriting the request.</li>
              <li>APIs such as <a href="/api/request-info" rel="nofollow">/api/request-info</a> remain HTTP-only as well.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="marketing-section">
            <h3>Data sources</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>ASN data is updated and gathered from https://github.com/ipverse/as-ip-blocks.</li>
              <li>IP address data is powered by IPLocate.io.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel--actions">
        <header class="panel__header">
          <h2>Keep working</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="actions-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Main diagnostics</h3>
            <p>See your live IP, headers, and counters on the HTTP-only dashboard.</p>
            <div class="code-snippet">
              <a class="copy copy--snippet" href="/">[ OPEN MAIN PAGE ]</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>JSON snapshot</h3>
            <p>Grab your current scheme, IP, and headers via the API for scripts or support.</p>
            <div class="code-snippet">
              <a class="copy copy--snippet" href="/api/request-info" rel="nofollow">[ CALL /api/request-info ]</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%- include('partials/support-block') %>
    </main>

    <%- include('partials/footer', {formattedGeneratedAt, generationTimeMs, footerLines, generatedAt}) %>
  </body>
</html>
